WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:05.650
"The Batman" is finally here, and based on
fan reaction and critical consensus, it will

00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:10.650
certainly stand the test of time as one of
the all-time great Dark Knight stories.

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:13.809
But some fans aren't amused by one major cameo.

00:00:13.809 --> 00:00:14.860
Spoilers ahead!

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:19.350
Now that "The Batman" is out and people have
had time to marinate on it for a little while,

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:20.900
it's not above criticism.

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:25.370
No movie is perfect, and superhero fans have
never had any problem letting their feelings

00:00:25.370 --> 00:00:28.360
be known when they believe a work of art can
do better.

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:32.430
And plenty of people have already taken to
Reddit to talk about that big cameo toward

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:33.430
the end of the film.

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:37.490
After Riddler is locked up in Arkham, he speaks
to his next-door cellmate.

00:00:37.490 --> 00:00:42.170
This individual never reveals a name, and
in the credits, they're listed as "Unseen

00:00:42.170 --> 00:00:45.990
Arkham Prisoner," but it's pretty obvious
who it's meant to be.

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:49.510
"You can call me 'Joker'!"

00:00:49.510 --> 00:00:55.010
The unseen Arkham prisoner, played by Barry
Keoghan, references clowns and laughs maniacally

00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:56.990
at the end of his speech.

00:00:56.990 --> 00:01:00.920
In any other superhero movie, this would be
a tease that the Joker will be the villain

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:02.079
in the sequel.

00:01:02.079 --> 00:01:06.280
But there are plenty of fans who believe the
sequel would be better suited with a different

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:07.280
antagonist.

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:11.120
One Redditor kickstarted a thread talking
about how there have been too many Jokers

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:12.720
as of late, saying,

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:18.120
"Batman has the most distinct and varied gallery
of villains and yet we are doing the Joker

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:19.120
again.

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:23.300
Barry Keoghan ([who] is great in anything)
is the third live action Joker in six years,

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:27.080
I wish we could get villains that weren't
properly explored in movies yet."

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:32.340
The Jokers he's referring to are Jared Leto
in "Suicide Squad" and "Zack Snyder's Justice

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:35.020
League," plus Joaquin Phoenix in "Joker."

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:40.150
It's a lot of the same, so to avoid people
burning out on Joker's shenanigans, some people

00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:43.880
believe "The Batman 2" would be better off
exploring new characters.

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:48.240
Still, there are those who thought Keoghan
could have been someone else entirely, with

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:49.780
one fellow Redditor writing,

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:54.150
"According to the teaser website from the
post-credits scene it seems that Barry Keoghan

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:56.119
might actually be Hush."

00:01:56.119 --> 00:02:01.120
Hush is an oft-ignored Batman rogue who's
actually Thomas Elliot, and he was hinted

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:02.549
at in the film itself.

00:02:02.549 --> 00:02:06.730
When Bruce Wayne learns what his father did
to a reporter, we discover the reporter's

00:02:06.730 --> 00:02:08.050
last name was Elliot.

00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:11.220
Now, we know Barry Keoghan won't be Hush.

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:13.920
Director Matt Reeves has already shot that
idea down.

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:19.230
Reeves revealed that the unseen Arkham prisoner
is, in fact, the Joker, albeit a prototype

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:22.850
version who may not necessarily be fully formed
at this point.

00:02:22.850 --> 00:02:27.690
It's possible Reeves just wanted audiences
to know that the Joker is in this universe,

00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:30.620
but he may not inherently be the "big bad"
of the sequel.

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:35.290
In the follow-up, perhaps we'll get a version
of a deleted scene, which Reeves describes

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:36.290
like this:

00:02:36.290 --> 00:02:40.470
"It's a scene where Batman is so unnerved
because the Riddler is writing to him.

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:43.020
And he's like, 'Well, why is this guy writing
to me?'

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:45.730
And he figures he's got to profile this killer.

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:50.110
He goes to see another killer that he's clearly
had an experience with in these first two

00:02:50.110 --> 00:02:51.110
years."

00:02:51.110 --> 00:02:55.240
The killer in question is the Joker, in a
scene that Reeves compares to "Silence of

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:56.240
the Lambs."

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:59.849
Could we see a scene like it play out in "The
Batman 2," where Bruce Wayne is at the end

00:02:59.849 --> 00:03:03.920
of his rope with Hush, so he turns to another
madman to stop him?

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:08.879
Batman has an expansive rogues gallery, and
fans have speculated a future villain could

00:03:08.879 --> 00:03:12.310
be anyone from Mr. Freeze to the Court of
Owls.

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:16.520
It's a big universe out there, and it's clear
the newest version of the Caped Crusader is

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:17.930
just getting started.

00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:22.440
There are many directions a sequel could go
in, and for a subset of fans, they'd love

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:24.209
to see the road less traveled.

